        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 5.2 Impurity criterion",
            "block_bbox": [
                139,
                242,
                470,
                273
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    139.0,
                    242.0
                ],
                [
                    470.0,
                    242.0
                ],
                [
                    470.0,
                    273.0
                ],
                [
                    139.0,
                    273.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Given the score ordering, a cut is scored by an impurity that combines, following Eriksson et al. (2009), a within-child response term, a within-child  $ t_{1} $-homogeneity term and a balance penalty, with weights A and B (defaults A = B = 0.3). The default response term adopted herein is Eriksson's within-child response variance. For problems with many responses compressed into few Y-side components a Y-latent variance is additionally provided that scores cuts in the reduced U-subspace; it coincides with the variance criterion when the number of retained Y-components matches the response rank and is a genuine reduction otherwise. This criterion, together with a slope-contrast objective, a kurtosis-based bimodality criterion and an impurity-invariant k-means partition in the joint  $ (t_{1}, u_{1}) $ score plane, is catalogued in the Supplementary Material. Two cut-search heuristics are available: exhaustive search scans every interior break of the  $ t_{1} $-order (the default), and a polynomial search exploits the piecewise-quadratic structure of the variance impurity for a search cost independent of n. The k-means partition, which clusters the rows in the  $ (t_{1}, u_{1}) $ plane and snaps the resulting boundary back to a threshold on  $ t_{1} $, is selected by a separate flag; it turns out to pair particularly well with the coskewness objective for regime recovery (Section 6).",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                289,
                1083,
                725
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    289.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
