        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Given a candidate cut partitioning $\\mathcal{N}$ into $\\mathcal{L}$, $\\mathcal{R}$, the gate compares the parent score $S_p = S(\\mathcal{N}; \\sigma_N^2)$ with the size-weighted combined child score $S_c = (|\\mathcal{L}| S(\\mathcal{L}; \\sigma_N^2) + |\\mathcal{R}| S(\\mathcal{R}; \\sigma_N^2)) / (\\lfloor \\mathcal{L} \\rfloor + |\\mathcal{R} \\rfloor)$ and accepts the split iff $(S_p - S_c) / S_p \\geq \\tau_{cv}$, with a default $\\tau_{cv} = 0.01$. Crucially, both children are scored with the parent's per-response variance $\\sigma_N^2$ as the common denominator: scoring each node against its own variance makes the scores incomparable across nodes, and on regime-jump data would spuriously reject the very root split that separates two regimes.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                1038,
                1114,
                1213
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
